- GGUF weights file, tensor dtypes, `build_moe_ffn`/`qwen35moe.cpp`/`llama-graph.cpp` (diffstat: untouched), ggml-cpu kernels, CUDA backend, sampler code, KV cache.
  Confirmed by `git diff cf34d3113 HEAD --stat` listing only the 9 files above. Not verified at run-time.
- Changed non-semantically: ggml sched CPU compute call structure (one extra indirection, views), loader advice list when `RANDOM=1` or prefetch, llama_mmap advice when `LLAMA_MMAP_ADVICE=random`.
- Not verified by me: that decode-time `-cmoe` graph on this model really assigns the entire MoE sub-block (ids, expert matmuls) to one CPU split such that `src[1]` is host-resident at callback; the code defensively disables pregate (`:796-802`) and fill (`:1162-1175`) otherwise, and the logs `expert_pregate: prediction disabled` / `expert_fill: inactive (unsupported host router ids)` indicate it.

## 8. Counters and what they actually measure
Demand fill (`expert_fill: stats`, summed over files; `llama-context.cpp:1256-1284`, struct `Counters` `llama-expert-fill.h:119`):
- `calls/ranges`: one call per (layer, file, ubatch) that fills; ranges = per-tensor-per-expert ranges.
- `bytes_requested`: page-aligned union per call (`:357`). `bytes_skipped_resident`: bytes of those pages that `mincore` said resident at fill start (`:364`). `bytes_read`/`read_syscalls`: bytes/syscalls actually `pread` by demand workers.
  `decode_bytes_read`, `prompt_bytes_read`: split by phase.
- `total_fill_wall_ns`/`decode_fill_wall_ns`: main-thread wall time inside `fill()` (job building, mincore, wait). `demand_wait_ns_total` equals `total_fill_wall_ns`; `demand_wait_ns_per_token = total / expert_demand_tokens` (decode tokens when MAX_TOKENS=1).
  It is blocked-on-IO time, not matmul time and not total decode time.
Pregate (`expert_pregate: stats`, `llama-context.cpp:871-893`), exact line:
`expert_pregate: stats predictions= arrivals= hits= actual= predicted= recall_at_k= precision= async_bytes_read= async_reads= async_errors= dropped= cancelled_jobs= cancelled_bytes= inflight_at_arrival= inflight_reads= pending_reads= sync_bytes_with_prediction= sync_bytes_without_prediction= sync_calls_with_prediction= sync_calls_without_prediction= skips= pred_done= pred_inflight= pred_queued= pred_resident_at_predict= hit_done= hit_inflight= hit_queued= hit_resident= recall_at_k_d1= recall_at_k_d2= actual_d1= actual_d2= hits_d1= hits_d2= demand_wait_ns_total= demand_wait_ns_per_token=`
- `predictions`: hook calls x distances (not per expert). `arrivals`: (layer, distance) pairs that had a prediction. `predicted`=K per arrival; `actual`= unique selected ids of that layer at arrival; `hits` = predicted ids that appear in `selected`.
  `recall_at_k = hits/actual`, `precision = hits/predicted`. These are set-overlap only; independent of IO.
- `pred_{done,inflight,queued,resident_at_predict}` classify every predicted expert at arrival (before demand): resident_at_predict (all its ranges were already in page cache by `mincore` at prediction),
  else queued (any chunk cancelled/not started/errored/dropped), inflight (some chunk running), done (no pending). `hit_*` = same buckets restricted to ids actually selected.
  `done` means every chunk's worker finished, including chunks skipped because the worker's own `mincore` found the pages resident; it does not re-check residency at demand time and cannot see eviction between completion and use.
- `async_bytes_read/async_reads`: all bytes preread by async workers, hit or not. `cancelled_*`: chunks cancelled before start. `dropped`: whole submissions dropped for the 4096-chunk bound.
- `sync_bytes_with_prediction` / `_without_prediction`: demand `pread` bytes in layers that had any prediction vs layers (including layer 0 and any non-predicted) that had none; calls likewise.
Diag (`LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_DIAG=1` only): `majflt_fill/minflt_fill` (process-wide getrusage during the demand fill window), `majflt_between/minflt_between` (process-wide between fill end and next fill / token end: includes compute-side page faults, mostly expert pages),
`thread_majflt/minflt` (fill worker threads, nonzero only with POPULATE), `post_nonresident` (non-resident pages right after demand fill; should be ~0), `pre_use_nonresident` (non-resident right before first matmul use), `next_nonresident` (previous layer's ranges probed when the next layer fills, i.e. eviction after use).
All are page counts of mincore, summed over probes.

**Answer to (a)/(b)/(c):**
- (a) "predicted the right expert": `hits`, `recall_at_k(_d1/_d2)`, `precision`. Directly measured; nothing else needed.
- (b) "its bytes were resident before demand": partly. Closest are `hit_done` (+`hit_inflight` partial), `hit_resident` (resident BEFORE any pregate IO, so not credited to pregate), and the change in `decode_bytes_read` / `bytes_skipped_resident` / `sync_bytes_with_prediction` versus a fill-only baseline run.
  `bytes_skipped_resident` alone cannot attribute residency to prediction versus natural page-cache retention from earlier tokens. `hit_done` is an I/O-state classification, not a residency measurement: it is not re-validated by `mincore` at arrival/demand; with `DIAG=1`, `pre_use_nonresident` is a residency measurement but only after the demand fill, and not split by predicted/unpredicted.
- (c) fewer page faults vs lower decode time: not separable without DIAG. Without DIAG there is no fault counter at all in the engine and no per-token or per-layer wall time split; `demand_wait_ns_per_token` captures only time blocked in `fill()`. Fault counts (`majflt_between`, `minflt_between`) exist only in DIAG runs (which add cuts, getrusage and mincore overhead).
  Note preads do not count as page faults, so a fill config can drive `majflt` to ~0 while moving the same (or more) IO into `fill()`; fewer faults must not be read as less IO.

**What is missing:**
1. Per-actual-expert residency at arrival (mincore of each *selected* expert's ranges, pre-demand), split by predicted vs not predicted: gives bytes-resident-before-demand attributable to prediction. Today only classification of predicted experts exists.
2. Wasted prefetch: bytes read for ids not selected (`async_bytes_read` minus bytes of hit experts); no counter. Also no counter of prefetched-then-evicted bytes.
3. Non-DIAG fault counters (e.g. `getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF)` start/end of generation, or `/proc/self/stat` delta) and per-token decode wall time; use `llama_perf` eval ms/token plus external `/proc/<pid>/stat` or `perf stat -e major-faults,minor-faults`.
4. Matmul-side stall time (time inside MUL_MAT_ID per layer) vs fill time, so "lower decode time" can be decomposed.
5. Time the demand fill spent waiting on in-flight async pages (duplicate IO / page-lock waits) and the time for the pregate hook itself (router dot + mincore on the critical path).
6. Page-cache pressure metrics (free/available MemAvailable, Cached) over the run for an 8 GB box.

## 9. Expert placement on the GPU
No. The diff adds no `ggml_backend_tensor_set/alloc`, buffer-type, CUDA or buffer allocation code (grep over `git diff cf34d3113 HEAD`, only `memcpy` for router floats and ids). `-cmoe` keeps `ffn_*_exps` in the CPU/mmap buffer
(`common/arg.cpp:2731`), and `expert_fill_init` explicitly throws if the expert tensors are not host mmap-backed (`llama-context.cpp:657-660`). Routers (`ffn_gate_inp`, on GPU) are never read from GPU memory; the predictor decodes its own CPU copy from the file (`:709-735`).
Page-cache residency is the only "placement" being changed.

## Bugs / questionable semantics
1. Pregate predicts layer L+1 from layer L's FFN input, not the real L+1 router input (skips L's MoE+shared-expert output, residual, L+1 attention+norm). Recall may be modest; check `recall_at_k_d1` before attributing speedups. LOOKAHEAD=2 is staler.
2. `ORDER=before|after` is largely moot for IO start time because async workers are gated by `demand_active_` (`llama-expert-fill.h:444`); `before` only moves the mincore probes/enqueue earlier. Running async chunks still compete with demand IO.
3. In-flight async pages look non-resident to `mincore`, so demand re-issues the read (duplicate syscalls; page-lock serialises) and `bytes_read`/`sync_bytes` include bytes the async side also read. `hit_inflight` experts therefore are not a clean "resident before demand".
4. `Arrival::done` counts a batch with zero bytes read (all chunks skipped as resident at worker time) as done, same as one that actually read; `pred_resident_at_predict` handles only prediction-time residency.
5. Layer 0 is never predicted (router copy loop starts at 1, `:710`) and layers where `il+1>=n_layer`, so `sync_*_without_prediction` includes layer 0 every token, not only mispredicted layers.
6. `pregate_sync_with` uses `!predicted.empty()`; `predicted` accumulates across distances, so with LOOKAHEAD=2 it is true for any layer that got either prediction.
7. `DIAG=1` observes every MUL_MAT_ID (`:1144-1149`) -> extra cuts + syscalls; DIAG timing is not comparable to non-DIAG timing. `majflt_*` use RUSAGE_SELF (whole process, including CUDA/other threads), not just expert-fault attribution.
8. PREGATE=1 with FILL=0 is silently inactive (init is inside `expert_fill_init`); `expert_config: effective ... pregate=1` is printed though pregate is not active. Engagement check `expert_pregate: active` guards this.
9. `ExpertConfig::read()` aborts on any unknown `LLAMA_EXPERT_*` env var, even with FILL=0 (and is parsed repeatedly: loader, mmap, model, context).
10. `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_RANDOM=1` adds expert ranges to the lazy-range list, which also turns off MAP_POPULATE for the whole file (`llama-mmap.cpp:509`), a side effect on non-expert pages; `decode` variant avoids it but applies MADV_RANDOM only from first decode token (prompt-phase mmap faults, if any, unaffected, and MAX_TOKENS=1 means no fill in prompt).
11. A prompt of one token is treated as decode (`n_tokens==1`), counts in decode stats and gets random advice.
12. `expert_demand_tokens` counts filled-ubatch tokens only; with MIN/MAX changes `demand_wait_ns_per_token` denominators change meaning.
13. Fill/pregate cost per layer on the critical path (mincore probes, scalar router dot, fill job build) scales with n_expert*n_embd and K; no timers cover it.
14. Component.yaml `env_ranges` text (e.g. THREADS 1..256) refers to patch 03; HEAD (patch 08) is strict 1..64, 64..65536 for chunk; `component.yaml` default `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_THREADS=16` is within range.
# MoE expert streaming / offloading: methods catalog for Orin Nano 8GB + llama.cpp mmap (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B)

Provenance legend: [V] = read from the page/paper text this session; [S] = search-snippet/secondary only; [M] = my background knowledge, not re-verified. Several arXiv PDFs (DALI, SpecPrefetch, kernel-managed tiering) were not readable by the fetch tool; their numbers are absent. Many 2026 arXiv IDs are preprints; treat numbers as unreviewed.

## 0. Bottom line

1. We are on the same board class and same model family as the closest prior art: anyesh's `moe-stream` llama.cpp fork on a Jetson Orin Nano Super 8GB streaming Gemma-4 26B-A4B (and Qwen3.6-35B-A3B runs) [V] https://dev.to/anyesh/running-a-26b-moe-on-an-8-gb-jetson-by-streaming-experts-from-ssd-2jpf . Their result: `--cpu-moe` mmap baseline 1.94 tok/s -> 2.95 tok/s with a user-space per-layer LFU slot pool (32 slots/layer, ~3 GB pinned, 8 IO threads, pread into slots) plus overlap of the shared dense FFN with disk reads (overlap off: 2.23; so cache ~+15%, overlap ~+32%). Bitwise identical logits verified with memcmp. They measured fio ~1.27 GB/s random reads on the same board and estimated an IO ceiling near 5 tok/s for their 26B model. They did NOT report hit rate (a commenter flagged this).
2. At ~586 MiB of expert bytes per token, the Orin Nano NVMe ceiling is ~2-5 tok/s with zero caching. We are at 1.2-1.4. Reaching 5-10 tok/s requires a cache hit rate of roughly 60-90% on a cache that can only be ~3 GiB (about 16% of expert bytes), so >5 tok/s is plausible only with excellent locality; 10 tok/s is unlikely.
3. When IO-bound, prediction/prefetch only reorders IO (hides it behind compute); it cannot beat the bandwidth ceiling. Only (a) fewer bytes read (higher hit rate, better cache policy) or (b) higher effective bandwidth (bigger contiguous reads, higher QD, fewer duplicate reads) raise the ceiling. Prefetch matters only to the extent compute time c is comparable to IO time.

## 1. Back-of-envelope bound

Per token: 40 layers x 8 experts x 1.83 MiB = 585.6 MiB = 614 MB (decimal). Non-expert weights are resident on the GPU.

Pure-NVMe ceiling (every expert byte read from SSD, perfect overlap, c = 0):

| Effective read BW | tok/s ceiling | Source for BW |
|---|---|---|
| 1.27 GB/s | 2.07 | fio random reads, same board class [V dev.to] |
| 1.55 GB/s | 2.5 | hdparm on Orin NVMe [S, NVIDIA forum / tmpdir thread] |
| 2.0 GB/s | 3.3 | StorageReview measured 1.7-2.5 GB/s on Orin Nano Super Gen3 x4 [S] https://www.storagereview.com/review/nvidia-jetson-orin-nano-super-powering-deepseek-r1-70b-inference-at-the-edge |
| 3.0 GB/s | 4.9 | Gen3 x4 practical max (~3.9 GB/s raw) [M] |

Our measured 1.2-1.4 tok/s = 0.71-0.83 s/token vs 0.47 s (at 1.27 GB/s) if every byte were read: so either hit rate is already modest-to-moderate and overlap is poor, or effective BW is much below fio. We do not know which; instrument first (decode_bytes_read / decode tokens vs 586 MiB).

Required hit rate h for target T. Miss bytes = (1-h) x 614 MB. Two models: "overlapped" = IO fully hidden behind compute (time/token = max(IO, c)); "serial" = IO then compute (time = IO + c). c = non-IO time per token, assumed 0.10 s (CPU expert matmul + GPU non-expert + sync; our actual c is unmeasured, the 586 MiB at ~30-60 GB/s is only 10-20 ms, so 0.05-0.10 s is a guess to measure).

Required hit rate (0% = no cache needed; "n/a" = infeasible):

| Target | BW 1.27 GB/s overlapped | 1.27 serial c=0.1 | 2.0 GB/s overlapped | 2.0 serial c=0.1 | 3.0 GB/s overlapped |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 tok/s | ~0% (2.07 ceiling) | 15% | 0% | 0% | 0% |
| 5 tok/s | 59% | 79% | 35% | 67% | 0% (ceiling 4.9: ~0%) |
| 10 tok/s | 79% | n/a (c=0.1 caps at 10; with c=0.05: 89%) | 67% | 84% (c=0.05) | 51% |

Formula: h = 1 - BW x (1/T - c_serial) / 614 MB (serial), h = 1 - BW/(T x 614 MB) (overlapped).

Cache capacity reality check: 7.4 GiB - 2.5 GiB non-expert - ~1.5 GiB (OS, CUDA ctx, KV, scratch, page-cache pressure) ~= 3 GiB for experts = ~1,680 experts = ~42/layer = 16% of 256. Published locality for a similar model (Qwen3-30B-A3B, 128 experts/layer, top-8): at 13.4% of experts cached, LRU hits 65.9%, LFU 60.1%, static pin 59.2%, Belady oracle 79.1% [S] https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.18261 . A 16% budget therefore suggests h ~ 0.6-0.7 realistic, 0.8 is oracle-level. Implication: 5 tok/s needs ~35-67% hit at >=2 GB/s (reachable), ~60-80% at 1.27 GB/s (borderline), 10 tok/s is out of reach without lossy tricks. Expect best case realistic ~3-4 tok/s, stretch 5. (Our current page cache is already doing implicit LRU on whatever fits, so the real headroom from a better cache policy is smaller than the table suggests: measure current h first.)

## 2. Catalog

### 2.1 Directly applicable, lossless

**A. User-space slot pool with LFU (anyesh moe-stream; also "Janas-LLM" SSD expert cache) [V]**
- Mechanism: per-layer pool of N expert-sized slots (persistent pinned tensor) + slot-id tensor; scheduler callback on the router top-k node reads ids; hits reuse slots; misses pread directly into assigned slots with a thread pool; ids remapped to slot indices ONLY for the main matmul (scales/biases/LoRA keep true ids; silent-degradation pitfall). Router top-k moved earlier so reads overlap with shared-expert FFN on GPU; one sync before expert matmul.
- Gains: Orin Nano Super 8GB, Gemma-4 26B Q4: 1.94 -> 2.95 tok/s (32 slots, 8 threads). 64 slots (~6 GB) starved the system. Peak RSS 6.7 GB. Cold prefill ~1/4 of baseline.
- Lossless: yes, bitwise logits (memcmp over 32 greedy steps).
- Prereqs: pinned (cudaHostAlloc / unified) slot memory; expert tensors addressable via ids remap.
- Mapping to us: we already own the hook point and pread pool (`expert_fill_selected`, ExpertFiller). Differences: they replace page cache with an explicit pool (deterministic residency, no minor faults, hugepage-able, LFU not kernel LRU, no pollution from prefetch of wrong experts). We would (i) allocate a pool (anonymous, MAP_HUGETLB/THP, or cudaHostAlloc), (ii) point MUL_MAT_ID at slots: either remap `ids` (as they do) or build per-layer expert "view" tensor and pass slot ids in a new ggml op/arg; (iii) pread misses straight into slots (O_DIRECT optional, avoids double residency of page cache + pool; critical on 8 GB: page cache copy + pool copy = 2x). Where: llama-graph.cpp (ids remap; note stock `moe_cache_size`/`build_moe_cache_slots` scaffolding already exists in cf34d311, see sourcemap section 1), llama-context.cpp callback, llama-expert-fill.h.
- Effort: high (2-3 days; correctness harness memcmp on logits). Biggest single lever; also kills minor faults (see D).
- Evidence they used: end-to-end llama-bench tg128, on/off ablation of overlap, bitwise logit identity; fio ceiling. Weak: no hit rate, no cold/warm disclosure.

**B. GPU-side expert cache with heat map (llama.cpp PR #26563 `-ehs`, closed/reopened #26824, continued #30059) [V]**
https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/26563 : decay-weighted usage heat map per layer, hot experts copied into a GPU store, cold experts computed on CPU via MUL_MAT_ID_COLD, hysteresis 1.3x + dwell to swap. Gains on discrete-GPU boxes: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 1.7-2.1x; Qwen3.5-122B regression 0.84x (1.21x with dwell 16); 2x4090 tester found static "pin whole layers" equal or better (near-uniform routing). Lessons: (1) benefit depends on routing skew; (2) swap churn costs more than it gains without hysteresis; (3) it needs all experts host-resident (not our case). Unified memory removes the copy, so only the heat-map/hot-pinning idea transfers: use the heat map to decide which experts to keep pinned (mlock) in the pool. Lossless-ish (author notes tiny variations at temp 0 when the expert split changes; with CPU compute of identical Q4 kernels vs CUDA kernels results can differ numerically; keep CPU-only compute to stay bit-identical). Effort: medium once A exists.

**C. Frequency-aware / Belady-approximating eviction (FlashMoE; replay-eval papers) [V]**
- FlashMoE (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.17063v1): tiny MLP scores eviction from recency+frequency, trained to mimic Belady; RTX 5070 Ti + PCIe5 SSD 7.4 GB/s; hit rate +21% vs LRU, +51% vs LFU (OLMoE-1B-7B), decode +22% (OLMoE) but only +7% (Qwen3-30B-A3B); expert load >70% of decode time (3 ms per expert vs 158 us FFN op). Lossless claim only by construction (no output equivalence test).
- Reproducible-eval paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07911): after fixing replay artifacts there is still a 44-46% gap to offline optimum, but lightweight causal predictors recover only ~11% of that gap; 84-97% of the gap is "which resident to evict". Realistic: LFU/LRU hybrids (ARC/LFRU) capture most of what is practical. Do not expect a learned eviction policy to rescue us.
- Qwen3-30B-A3B locality [S, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.18261]: next-token reuse ~2x random baseline, 64-token reuse 95-99% in long contexts; top expert sets across domains overlap Jaccard as low as 0.066 (code vs others), so an offline pin list from one workload transfers poorly. At tiny budget (8 slots) LRU 0.5% vs LFU 12.7%.
- Mapping: policy lives in the slot pool (A). In today's page-cache design, we can emulate frequency pinning by mlock-ing frequently hit expert ranges (mlock on mmap pages after fill) and `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)`/`madvise(MADV_COLD/PAGEOUT)` for cold ones. Effort: low-medium. Metric: hit rate vs cache size curve from a trace replay (log expert ids per token/layer once, simulate LRU/LFU/ARC offline in Python at 42 slots/layer before building anything). That simulation is the cheapest, highest-information next step.

**D. Eliminate page-fault / TLB overhead of mmap (Flash-MoE, ours) [V]**
Flash-MoE (https://lilting.ch/en/articles/flash-moe-qwen35-397b-metal-inference): mmap ran ~0.2x of pread-based custom path on Apple Silicon due to fault overhead; relying on OS page cache gave ~71% hit; prefetching and LZ4 made things WORSE; predictive routing only 31% accurate; SSD DMA and GPU compete for the unified memory controller so heavy IO parallelism hurt, a mostly serial pipeline was best; peak SSD 17.5 GB/s, per-layer 4.28 ms of which 2.41 ms IO. 586 MiB/token = ~150k 4 KiB pages/token; at ~0.3-1 us per minor fault (kernel 5.15 arm64) that is ~45-150 ms/token of pure fault cost even with 100% page-cache hits. Our patches avoid major faults but not minor faults (sourcemap section 4/8: pread populates page cache but not PTEs). Options: MADV_POPULATE_READ (patch 05 POPULATE=1; costs a second pass), larger `fault_around_bytes` (system-wide debugfs, default 64 KiB), THP for file pages (not for ext4 page cache), or a user-space anonymous hugepage pool (A). Lossless. Effort: low (test POPULATE, perf stat minor-faults) to high (pool). Metric: `perf stat -e minor-faults,major-faults` per token, and time inside MUL_MAT_ID at 100% warm cache (upper bound on what any IO scheme can reach).

**E. Overlap IO with GPU work (anyesh; Fate; Pre-gated MoE) [V for anyesh, Fate]**
Router top-k first, issue reads, run shared-expert / independent GPU work meanwhile, join before expert matmul. anyesh: +32% (2.23 -> 2.95). In our graph the shared expert is in `build_moe_ffn`'s caller (qwen35moe.cpp build_layer_ffn) on GPU while routed experts are on CPU split; today the callback blocks the main thread in `fill()` before the CPU split's first MUL_MAT_ID, and the GPU split for shared expert is ordered by the scheduler. Need to check whether the scheduler runs the shared-expert GPU split before or after the CPU MoE split (sourcemap does not say). Change: reorder graph so shared-expert FFN is enqueued async on GPU before the fill wait, or move `fill` to a non-blocking "start" at router-output time and "join" before MUL_MAT_ID. Lossless. Effort: medium.

**F. Cross-layer prediction improvements (Fate, ETH pre-attention, Mixtral-offloading, Pre-gated) [V/S]**
- Mixtral-offloading (Eliseev & Mazur, https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17238) [S]: LRU cache + apply next layers' gates to current hidden state ("speculative loading", ~80% 1 layer ahead, ~90% for... slide-deck figures, unverified), mixed quantization; 2-3 tok/s on T4/3060/3080M. Quantization is the lossy part, caching/speculation lossless.
- Fate (https://arxiv.org/html/2502.12224v1) [V]: exactly our predictor (layer i gate input -> layer i+1 gate): decode accuracy 78.8% (vs attention input of i+1: 84.0%; attention input of i: 76.9%); prefill >99% (not applicable); expanding to experts above the 75th percentile of confidence gives 97.2% recall; shallow-favoring cache (layers 0-3 fully cached, ARC) hits 99% in layers 0-3; lossy part is INT2 for low-popularity experts (accuracy -0.15 to -1.8 points). Hardware RTX 3090/1080 Ti, PCIe 3.0 x16. Up to 3.7x decode vs load-on-demand.
- ETH pre-attention predictor (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10676v1) [V]: same-layer top-k predicted from pre-attention norm output by 2 linear layers (~0.15 ms vs attention 0.74-1.13 ms); exact-match 93-98% first layer, top-1 98-99.5%; but overlap window is only the attention duration, over-provisioning loads ~67% more experts; no end-to-end measurement. For us the window (attention only, ~1 ms) is far shorter than an expert load (~12 ms for 8 experts at 1.27 GB/s), so useless alone; only useful as a more accurate L+1 predictor if lookahead of a full layer is supplied.
- Our setup: pregate uses L FFN input -> L+1 router, recall_at_k_d1 should be ~Fate's 79% (measure!). Layer 0 is never predicted. Improvements: (i) over-provision K to 10-12 (Fate-style confidence expansion) only if IO headroom exists; (ii) train a tiny per-layer linear correction on a trace (two-linear predictor from L's input to L+1's top-8; training data = logged router outputs); (iii) union with the previous token's L+1 set (temporal locality); (iv) layer-0: predict from previous token's layer-0 set / hot list. Lossless: yes (predictor never alters selection). Effort: low-medium. Metric: recall@8 AND, more importantly, "resident before demand" (see section 4).

**G. Row-column bundling analogue: contiguous per-expert layout (Apple LLM in a flash) [V/S]**
https://arxiv.org/html/2312.11514v3 : bundle up/down rows, 32 KiB reads for OPT-6.7B, windowing (keep last 5 tokens' active neurons), load ~2% of FFN; 4-5x CPU / 20-25x GPU (v1) or 4x/7x/20x (v3) over naive; combined effect, no isolated ablation. MoE analogue: gate/up/down expert tensors are three separate tensors in GGUF, each with experts contiguous, so one expert = three ~0.6 MiB reads in three file regions (and the sourcemap confirms ranges are built per tensor). Offline re-layout of the GGUF so each expert's gate+up+down are one contiguous ~1.83 MiB block (also optionally layer-adjacent) means one pread per expert, fewer syscalls, larger sequential NVMe requests (NVMe throughput rises with request size), simpler O_DIRECT alignment. Tensor views: keep three ggml tensors with `nb[2]` = bundle stride and data offset to the part, so MUL_MAT_ID is unchanged (CPU kernel only needs nb02 and row strides). "Windowing" = the cache in A/C. Lossless (byte-identical weights, only placement). Effort: medium (a gguf rewriting script + loader support for non-contiguous per-tensor strides; check ggml asserts on `nb[2]` vs `ne[0]*ne[1]` size). Metric: fio at 0.6 MiB vs 1.8 MiB vs 4 MiB request sizes on our drive, and effective pread GB/s in `decode_bytes_read / decode_fill_wall_ns`.

**H. IO path tuning: chunking, queue depth, O_DIRECT, io_uring, readahead [S/M]**
- Parasail: O_DIRECT made cold start fast but warm start slow; io_uring async fixed both (https://parasail.io/blogs/making-cold-start-latencies-go-brrrr-a-multi-pronged-approach). Conviva: io_uring+O_DIRECT initially 60% slower than mmap; mmap peak 3.4 GB/s vs 21.7 GB/s fio on 32-drive array, fix was memory population/hugepages. Buffered vs O_DIRECT ~equal at cold cache (~7 GB/s on 990 Pro); QD1 15k IOPS vs QD128 1M IOPS [S, unverified secondary]. Flash-MoE: more IO parallelism can hurt on unified memory.
- Our 16 workers x 2 MiB pread buffered: sweep THREADS (4-32), CHUNK (0.5-4 MiB), O_DIRECT vs buffered, `blockdev --setra` (irrelevant w/ MADV_RANDOM+pread), scheduler none, and measure fio ceiling per request size first. O_DIRECT into a user-space pool avoids page cache double residency (matters at 8 GB; anyesh listed it as next step). Lossless. Effort: low for sweeps, medium for O_DIRECT (alignment: 4 KiB for offset/length/buffer; expert ranges are tensor-offset based, GGUF alignment is 32 B so edges need padding reads).

**I. Kernel-managed vs user-managed cache; hugepages; mlock [S/M]**
"Who Should Own the Expert Cache? Kernel-Managed Tiering" https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.12103 (PDF unreadable here; title/abstract suggest a kernel-vs-user cache comparison). Flash-MoE measured ~71% hit with plain OS page cache and chose it over a custom cache (on Apple SSD with 17 GB/s, where misses are cheap). On our slow SSD (~1.3-2 GB/s) misses are 10x more costly, so explicit policy (A/C) likely pays more than on Apple. mlock hot experts is the cheap version. Check RLIMIT_MEMLOCK and that pinned pages don't starve the GPU (unified memory, CUDA allocator shares the same pool; anyesh found 6 GB pool starved the system).

### 2.2 Lossless but low expected value for us

**J. Speculative decoding with MoE** [S]: MoE-Spec (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.16052v1): verification cost rises with draft-tree size because unique experts per layer approach all experts (256 here); proposes capping experts/layer (lossy if it changes verification). SP-MoE (Oct 2025): draft model drives prefetch + cutoff layer. ECOSPEC: cost-aware draft tree favoring already-loaded experts. DraftExpert (2607.24434): self-speculative, router-agreement predicts verifier experts. "The Limits of Speculation" 2609.22156. Strict greedy-verified SD is lossless in output. For batch size k tokens, unique experts/layer ~ min(256, 8k x (1-overlap)); with k=4 expect ~20-26 unique experts/layer = 2.5-3.2x bytes for up to ~4x tokens at best acceptance, so best case ~1.3x and negative at realistic acceptance (~60%) plus needs a draft model in the 7.4 GiB. Skip until IO side is solved. Effort: high.

**K. Fiddler/KTransformers/llama.cpp -ot, --n-cpu-moe [M/S]**
Fiddler computes non-resident experts on the CPU instead of moving weights (over 3 tok/s for Mixtral-8x7B on 24GB GPU) [S]. That is already our regime (CPU computes experts from host memory; unified memory means no PCIe copy at all). KTransformers is the same idea with optimized AMX/AVX kernels and expert placement [M]. For us the relevant knob is that CPU compute of 586 MiB (~15-60 ms) is not the bottleneck; IO is. ik_llama.cpp's MoE work (fused MoE up/gate, `-rtr` run-time repack, better quant kernels, `-fmoe`) [M, not researched] could shave c but does not address bytes. Placing experts on GPU (`-ot`/`--n-cpu-moe N` for part of layers) is moot because everything GPU-resident competes for the same 7.4 GiB; however with fixed budget, mixing "pin 3 GiB of whole-layer experts permanently" (e.g., ~6 full layers, 468 MiB each) is the static baseline the 2x4090 tester found competitive; for skewed routing per-expert pinning (C) wins, for near-uniform routing whole-layer pinning is simple and removes ~15% of layer reads deterministically.

**L. fMoE/FineMoE, MoE-Infinity, ProMoE, Klotski, DAOP, EdgeMoE, SwapMoE, SiDA** [S]
- fMoE (https://arxiv.org/html/2502.05370v2): "expert map" of per-iteration gate distributions + prompt semantic embeddings guide prefetch/cache; -47% latency, +36-39% hit rate vs SOTA (multi-GPU serving; not batch-1 edge).
- MoE-Infinity: request-level activation tracing (sparsity-aware cache); "2-20x" latency claims, batch-friendly serving [S, unverified].
- ProMoE (https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22134): predictor on intermediate results + prefetcher (chunked, async) into GPU cache; 2.20x prefill / 2.07x decode average (up to 3.21x/5.02x) on edge devices, integrated in llama.cpp; vs static caches and LRU [S]. Closest "edge + llama.cpp" academic prior art; read the full paper for its prefetch chunking and predictor (learned, uses intermediate activations) before building F(ii).
- Klotski, DAOP, EdgeMoE, SwapMoE, SiDA: not retrieved this session; DAOP and Fiddler are CPU-compute-for-misses designs, SiDA/SwapMoE are offline-predicted/lossy (SwapMoE keeps a fixed "important expert" set and substitutes). Treat as follow-ups.
- DALI (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.03495, local PC workload-aware offloading) and SpecPrefetch (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.24787, parameter-efficient prefetch heads): PDFs unreadable; abstract-level only.

### 2.3 Lossy (note only; violates our greedy-identical requirement)
- HOBBIT (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.01433): replace cache-miss experts with INT4/INT2 low-precision copies; <=1% accuracy loss; up to 9.93x decode vs SOTA; evaluated incl. Jetson AGX Orin 32GB with SSD loading (int8 base because fp16 too slow to load) [S]. Metrics: prefill latency (s) and decode tok/s.
- SMoE importance-driven scheduling (https://arxiv.org/html/2508.18983v2): substitute low-score uncached experts with similar cached ones; -24% to -48% TPOT, hit rate >60%, "nearly lossless" (accuracy -2.4 pts worst at large alpha) [V].
- Fate INT2 for unpopular experts (-0.15 to -1.8 points) [V].
- Cache-conditional routing / cache-aware router adaptation (https://arxiv.org/html/2412.00099v2 "Mixture of Cache-Conditional Experts for Efficient Mobile Device Inference"; https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.04895; training routers for locality: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.18261, a pre-registered negative result) [S]: bias routing toward resident experts. Large speedups on paper but changes outputs.
- Expert skipping / top-k reduction, MoE-Spec expert budgeting, mixed-precision GGUF (e.g., Q2 experts) [S/M].

## 3. Ranked shortlist: top 8 LOSSLESS ideas to prototype

Ordering is by (expected tok/s gain x confidence) / effort. Step 0 (instrumentation) precedes all.

**Step 0 (prerequisite, not ranked): measure the real situation.** (a) fio on the NVMe with 0.6 / 1.8 / 4 MiB reads, QD 1..32, buffered and O_DIRECT, with the whole 20.8 GiB file region (random offsets across the experts file), cold cache (`echo 3 > drop_caches`) -> true BW ceiling B. (b) Log per-token, per-layer expert ids for ~500 decode tokens over 3+ workloads; offline-replay LRU/LFU/ARC/Belady at 16-64 slots per layer. (c) From existing stats: decode_bytes_read per token vs 586 MiB = current miss fraction; decode_fill_wall_ns/token.

1. **Trace-replay simulation then frequency-aware residency (C -> A/I).** Hypothesis: with ~42 experts/layer budget, LFU/ARC-hybrid residency has hit rate >= 60% vs the page cache's unknown LRU-like value; metric: hit rate per policy on logged traces, then `decode_bytes_read/token` and tok/s. Cheap form first: mlock hot experts / DONTNEED cold ones in the current mmap design; expensive form: slot pool (A). Expected best case at B = 2 GB/s and h = 0.65: 614 MB x 0.35 / 2 GB/s = 0.11 s IO -> ~5-6 tok/s if overlapped, ~3-4 serial with c = 0.1. At B = 1.27: ~0.17 s -> 3.5-5 tok/s.
2. **IO <-> GPU overlap (E).** Hypothesis: shared-expert/non-expert GPU work can hide 20-40% of IO wait (anyesh +32%). Metric: demand_wait_ns_per_token before/after, tok/s. Best case: tok/s +25-35% at fixed hit rate.
3. **Per-expert contiguous bundling of gate/up/down in a rewritten GGUF (G).** Hypothesis: one 1.83 MiB read per expert instead of three 0.6 MiB reads raises effective BW by 10-40% (depends on fio curve). Metric: bytes_read / fill wall ns, read_syscalls per token. Best case: ceiling rises from ~2.1 to ~2.5-2.9 tok/s at the no-cache bound.
4. **Remove duplicate and wasted IO (sourcemap bugs #3/#4; wasted-prefetch counter).** Hypothesis: in-flight async reads get re-issued by demand (duplicate IO) and wrong predictions waste bandwidth in an IO-bound regime; fix: in-flight table shared by demand and async, prefetch only when IO queue idle, cap by recall confidence. Metric: async_bytes_read - bytes of hit experts (new counter); total bytes read per token should approach (1-h) x 586 MiB. Best case: 10-25% fewer bytes.
5. **Kill minor faults / PTE cost (D): POPULATE_READ, then hugepage user-space pool.** Hypothesis: ~150k minor faults per token cost 50-150 ms. Metric: `perf stat -e minor-faults` per token, MUL_MAT_ID time at 100% warm cache. Best case: 0.05-0.1 s/token saved: large when c dominates after IO improvements (at 5 tok/s the whole token budget is 200 ms).
6. **Better lookahead predictor (F):** over-provision/confidence expansion, learned linear correction from L input to L+1 top-8, union with previous-token set, layer-0 hot list. Hypothesis: recall@8 from ~79% (Fate baseline) to ~90%+ at <=+25% extra reads. Metric: recall_at_k_d1 AND new "selected experts resident before demand" fraction (Section 4). Best case: converts misses to overlapped reads; useful only when IO is not saturated, so rank is after 1-4.
7. **IO tuning sweep (H):** THREADS, CHUNK, QD, buffered vs O_DIRECT (+io_uring), alignment. Hypothesis: 16x2 MiB buffered is not at fio ceiling and contends with GPU memory traffic (Flash-MoE: parallelism can hurt on unified memory). Metric: GB/s per demand fill. Best case: 10-30% BW.
8. **Static layer-wise or shallow-favoring pinning as a baseline/fallback (K/Fate):** pin experts of the first layers (Fate: layers 0-3 reach 99% hit) or hottest per-layer sets from a profile; hypothesis: layer 0 (never predicted) and shallow layers are the worst demand-wait; metric: demand wait per layer (add per-layer timers). Best case: removes 1/40 to 3/40 of IO per token, i.e. 2.5-7%; weak alone, cheap.

Lower priority: speculative decoding (J), predictors that need the real attention (ETH) because the lookahead window is too short at IO-bound speeds.

## 4. Methodology lessons (what the papers do, and pitfalls)

- Separate prefill from decode: Fate, ProMoE, FlashMoE, HOBBIT all report prefill latency (s) and decode tok/s separately; prefill loads nearly all experts (batch union), decode is batch-1 and bandwidth-bound. We only fill in decode (MAX_TOKENS=1): report TTFT with and without it. anyesh: cold prefill ~1/4 baseline speed with streaming.
- Prediction accuracy is not readiness. Fate reports prefetch accuracy (78.8% -> 97% with expansion) but systems papers' real figure of merit is hit rate and stall time. Report all four: recall@k (predicted vs actual), precision (waste), "resident before demand" (mincore of actual selected experts at arrival, split by predicted/not predicted - missing counter #1 in sourcemap section 8), and stall time (demand_wait per token). ETH's paper is an example of the pitfall: great accuracy, expected-latency numbers are back-of-envelope not end-to-end.
- Hit rate must be paired with bytes: Flash-MoE's 71% page-cache hit rate looked fine but mmap ran at 0.2x because misses go through faults; FlashMoE's hit-rate gains (+21% vs LRU) translated to only +7-22% tok/s.
- Cache-policy evaluation traps (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07911): replay semantics (per-access vs fused per-event) inflate recency policies by 27-29% and can invert rankings; probe prompts that share one instruction template create identical prefixes and shift early-window results by 19-32 points; use multiple workloads (code vs prose differ: Jaccard 0.066), long generations (64-token reuse rises 95%->99% at long context), report per-step unique-experts-union relative to per-layer capacity rather than normalized miss fraction.
- Cold vs warm page cache: a flat-out different regime. Warm numbers on a 64 GB host mean the whole expert set is cached (InsiderLLM, https://buttondown.com/insiderllm/archive/nineteen-gigabytes-of-my-3090-sat-empty-the-3060/ [S]); on 8 GB it cannot. Always `drop_caches` + fixed warm-up tokens, report tokens 1-32 separately from steady state; run the same prompt twice and discard first. Record MemAvailable/Cached through the run (sourcemap missing #6). Watch that `LLAMA_EXPERT_FILL_DIAG=1` perturbs timing; use `perf stat` or getrusage deltas for fault counts instead.
- IO bandwidth ceilings: measure with fio at the actual request size/pattern; Orin Nano numbers vary 1.27 (fio random) / 1.55 (hdparm) / 1.7-2.5 GB/s (StorageReview) GB/s; SoC DMA contends with GPU/CPU on the same LPDDR5 (Flash-MoE). Drives rated Gen4 run at Gen3 on Orin.
- Per-token variance: decode time per token is bimodal (hit vs miss layers). Report median and p95 per-token ms, plus a time series, not just mean tok/s; llama-bench tg128 hides warm-up. Use identical greedy prompts and check output identity (memcmp logits as anyesh does, or at least identical text over 256+ tokens) after every change; anyesh's pitfall: remapping ids for the wrong consumer (scale/bias/LoRA) silently degrades quality.
- Lossy methods must report accuracy (MMLU/GSM8K/HumanEval, OpenCompass) and still often show output divergence at temperature 0 (PR #26563 author concedes tiny differences); that is why we exclude them.
- Compare against a proper baseline on the same board: llama.cpp `--cpu-moe` mmap (1.94 tok/s for Gemma-4 26B on Nano Super per anyesh) not an in-RAM baseline.

